## Treatment plan (plan of care) requirement

For outpatient specialized therapy, [wrong source: NC Medicaid requires the provider to obtain prior approval before providing the service, subject to listed exclusions, and the request must clearly show that a licensed therapist’s service is required.]

A treatment plan must be established after the evaluation and before treatment begins. It must be developed with the medical provider and the beneficiary; for children, with the parent(s) or legal guardian(s); and, when applicable, with an adult’s authorized representative. It must address functioning in clinical and natural environments, be culturally appropriate, and be a specific document based on the evaluation.

The plan must include short- and long-term functional goals and specific objectives drawn from the evaluation. The goals and objectives must be periodically reviewed and must seek functional, measurable outcomes.

Together, the treatment plan and written evaluation or re-evaluation report must contain:

- the plan’s start and end dates, for a duration of no more than six months;
- the discipline-specific treatment diagnosis and related medical diagnoses;
- rehabilitative or habilitative potential;
- defined, specific, measurable goals for each therapy discipline that can reasonably be achieved during the plan period;
- the skilled interventions, methods, procedures, and programs to be used;
- service frequency;
- the length of each visit in minutes; and
- the name, credentials, and signature of the professional completing the plan, dated on or before the plan start date.

All services must follow a plan meeting these requirements, and the provider must review and renew or revise the plan and goals at least every six calendar months.

